ere critical O-ring components were not fully understood under all operating conditions, leading to catastrophic system breakdown. Blue-collar workers face inexplicable algorithmic decisions impacting their work, schedules, and employment; SME owners cannot audit or trust opaque AI tools. The Directive must mandate explainability requirements and human-in-the-loop protocols with clear override authority for workers and supervisors. Germany's co-determination laws offer a strong existing framework for such worker representation in oversight, supported by labor organizations and consumer protection groups.\n\nAllowing unconstrained AI deployment without these foundational guardrails carries a significant probability of systemic failures, leading to widespread job precarity and economic instability. Economic models predict cascading failures in highly automated logistics networks without robust error handling. The 2008 financial crisis demonstrated how interconnected, opaque systems, left to self-regulate, triggered systemic collapse, impacting millions globally. Entire communities reliant on manufacturing and logistics in Germany and France face widespread unemployment and social unrest if this occurs. The Directive must establish harm-volume-indexed tiered oversight, with higher-risk AI systems facing stringent pre-deployment impact assessments and continuous monitoring. National labor inspectorates, like France's Inspection du Travail, require expanded mandates and technical expertise for enforcement. Broad public support for economic stability will drive this, countering industry lobbies pushing for deregulation.",
